WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
In this lesson we'll spend a few minutes going over session continuity. What it takes for an

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.960
agent to pick up exactly where it left off after an interruption. We'll go through checkpoint

3
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.639
design, failure recovery, state versioning for handling conflicts, and memory hygiene

4
00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:24.000
for keeping stored state clean. Let's get into it. An agent gets killed halfway through a long task.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.559
What happens next is entirely determined by decisions you made before it started.

6
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.080
Session resumption is the ability to pick a task back up from a saved checkpoint

7
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:40.720
without repeating completed steps or losing prior results. A checkpoint is that saved snapshot,

8
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.400
a record of task progress written at a defined phase boundary, giving the agent a known good

9
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.599
state to recover to. State versioning is the practice of tracking which version of the

10
00:00:51.599 --> 00:00:56.560
environment a checkpoint was written against, so the agent can tell when the world moved on

11
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:02.000
while it was down. Resumption depends on checkpoints, and checkpoints stay trustworthy

12
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.080
only when they carry version information. For session resumption to work, the agent needs three

13
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.360
things available when it restarts. First, it needs to know which steps have already been completed

14
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.040
and confirmed so it doesn't repeat work that already happened. Second, it needs the current

15
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:25.279
inputs that the next step requires to proceed. Without those, it can't continue even if it knows

16
00:01:25.279 --> 00:01:31.760
where it is. Third, it needs the accumulated results from prior steps that downstream phases

17
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.440
still depend on. If any of these three are missing, the agent is stuck. It either has to repeat

18
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.959
completed work, which can cause duplicate side effects, or restart from the beginning entirely.

19
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:48.959
Designing session resumption means ensuring that all three of these are written to durable storage

20
00:01:48.959 --> 00:01:55.599
before any interruption could plausibly occur. Checkpoint timing is its own decision, separate

21
00:01:55.599 --> 00:02:00.800
from what you store in one. The right pattern is to save at the end of each major phase,

22
00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:07.440
not after every individual tool call. Phase boundaries are natural resting points. The work

23
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.759
so far is coherent, and the next chunk has a clean place to start from. The second rule matters more

24
00:02:13.759 --> 00:02:20.000
than people expect. Write the checkpoint before taking any irreversible action in the next phase,

25
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.479
never after. If you write it after, a failure and a restart can replay that action a second time,

26
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:32.320
and irreversible means exactly what it says. You don't get to undo the duplicate. Sending the email

27
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.479
twice, charging the card twice, get the ordering wrong, and recovery becomes the thing that causes

28
00:02:38.479 --> 00:02:46.320
the damage. So what actually goes into a checkpoint? Four things, at minimum. A phase marker recording

29
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.880
which phase completed successfully, so that on resume the agent skips every phase before it,

30
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.479
rather than replaying them. The step outputs that downstream phases depend on, because a later phase

31
00:02:58.479 --> 00:03:06.160
can't run on results it no longer has. The original task inputs, so the agent can rebuild full context

32
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.880
rather than inferring what it was asked to do. And a time stamp for when the checkpoint was

33
00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.320
written, which gives you ordering when multiple checkpoints exist, and gives you a basis for

34
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:22.720
conflict detection later. Miss any one of these, and resumption becomes guesswork, which usually

35
00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:30.559
means repeated work, or a silent gap in the result. The granularity of checkpointing is a real design

36
00:03:30.559 --> 00:03:36.960
trade-off. Coarse checkpoints saved at phase boundaries are simpler. Fewer writes, less storage

37
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.679
complexity, easier to reason about. The downside is that on failure, the agent may have to redo some

38
00:03:43.679 --> 00:03:50.320
work within the phase it was executing. For most workflows, that cost is acceptable. Fine-grained

39
00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.399
checkpoints saved after every individual step minimize the repeated work on recovery, but they

40
00:03:56.399 --> 00:04:02.800
require many more writes, produce more complex state to manage and validate, and add latency

41
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.639
throughout execution. Fine-grained checkpointing is justified when individual steps are expensive

42
00:04:08.639 --> 00:04:15.360
to rerun, have external side effects, or involve writes to systems that don't support idempotent

43
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:21.839
retry. For everything else, coarse checkpoints are the better default. Don't add complexity you don't

44
00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:27.839
need. Recovery starts with a consistent pattern on session start. Before beginning any fresh

45
00:04:27.839 --> 00:04:34.000
execution, the agent checks whether an existing checkpoint is present. If one exists, it validates

46
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.839
the checkpoint's integrity before trusting it. A corrupt or incomplete checkpoint is worse than no

47
00:04:39.839 --> 00:04:45.200
checkpoint, because it can cause the agent to resume from a broken state without realizing it.

48
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:51.279
Once integrity is confirmed, the agent loads the checkpoint and skips all phases already marked as

49
00:04:51.279 --> 00:04:57.519
completed. Execution resumes at the first incomplete phase with the saved inputs and accumulated

50
00:04:57.519 --> 00:05:04.000
results loaded into context. A session that skips this check restarts from scratch every time there's

51
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.760
a failure. And if the checkpoint only covers part of the work, resume from what it does cover and

52
00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:17.279
flag the gap. Partial state is almost always better than no state. State versioning addresses a

53
00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:22.799
specific problem. The world can change between when a checkpoint was saved and when it's read

54
00:05:22.799 --> 00:05:29.920
back during recovery. A document might have been updated. An external resource might have changed.

55
00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:35.760
A configuration value might have shifted. If the agent resumes from a checkpoint and acts on state

56
00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.600
that no longer reflects reality, it can make incorrect decisions with high confidence. State

57
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.480
versioning prevents this by recording a time stamp and an environment hash at checkpoint

58
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:52.320
write time. On resume, the agent compares the current environment against the saved version.

59
00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.760
If they don't match, the agent flags the conflict and waits for a decision rather than silently

60
00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.839
proceeding. Silent resumption on stale state is a harder failure mode to diagnose than a detected

61
00:06:03.839 --> 00:06:11.040
conflict that pauses execution for review. Memory hygiene is the practice of actively pruning state

62
00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.559
that is no longer relevant to the task. In long-running agentic sessions, both the context

63
00:06:16.559 --> 00:06:24.160
window and external stores accumulate content over time. Earlier tool results, prior conversation

64
00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:29.920
turns, and intermediate outputs from completed phases all take up space. Some of it is still

65
00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:36.239
needed. Some of it is stale and can mislead the agent if left in place. The pruning decision comes

66
00:06:36.239 --> 00:06:42.239
down to a couple of checks. Is this state still relevant to current or future phases? And if it's

67
00:06:42.239 --> 00:06:48.160
being removed from context, does it need to be archived to an external store first? Prune from

68
00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:54.720
context to free up token budget. Prune from external stores to reduce retrieval noise. But

69
00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:00.239
always confirm before removing that the content either has no future value or has been written

70
00:07:00.239 --> 00:07:07.440
somewhere safe before deletion. An agent that crashes and restarts from turn zero doesn't just

71
00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.600
lose time. It has to redo all the work that you already paid for, and it can repeat actions that

72
00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:18.880
were only supposed to happen once. Checkpoints are what turn that restart into a resume.

73
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.959
Inversioning is what keeps the resume from redoing work or acting on things that moved

74
00:07:24.959 --> 00:07:29.440
on without it. That closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

